+| --- | --- |
+| **16070**, **DCO Chat Log** on both faces. NYC extraction gives **DOO Chat Log**. | **NCT0016070–NCT0016217**, tab **7**, CONR DCO. |
+| **16005**, CONR chat log. | **NCT0016005–NCT0016069**, tab **6**, CONR chat. |
+| **15939**, CONR Consolidated Ops chat log. The separate question sheet also gives **15939**. | **NCT0015939–NCT0016004**, tab **5**, CONR CCO. |
+| **53160**, Team 8 face and extraction give **Intel Chat Log**. The NYC label is obscured, and its extraction reads `f** fc*fc*£hat Log`. | No exact range established in the three targeted rows. |
+
+The three index start numbers match the short identifiers. The displayed inclusive spans are **148**, **65** and **66** labels in table order. The index reader includes **25-JuW3**, **25-Jul-03** and **25-JUI-03** in lines 2025–2029, and **25-Jul-03** on the three repeated rows at lines 2696–2700. The rows bear a historical **Secret** field. The scan faces, native index and current release status of the logs require separate verification. The ranges and staff email establish retrieval targets. [Public reader of the Commission document index](https://www.scribd.com/document/15556058/DM-B3-DOD-2-of-2-Fdr-All-Document-Indexes-in-File-272)
+
+The same email names the September 17 message as **53404–05** and three pages of notes as **53401–03**. The already held September message scan bears **NCT0053404**, a visible match to one of the cited numbers. It retains **1342Z** in the reply about Flight 77, **1408Z** for the Flight 93 inquiry and **1415Z** for a separate crash-report question. The January writer interprets the September exchange as supporting Boston as the source of Flight 77 notice. The January writer separately guesses who wrote the three pages of notes. Keep both interpretations attributed. The held single page leaves the counterpart **53405**, the notes and the source events unresolved. NYC pp. 2–3, Team 8 p. 4 lines 10–19, September scan p. 1 lines 16–18 and 47–56. [September correspondence comparison](https://github.com/AveLyra/911-conspiracy-investigation/blob/73da6912b3b586cb90823c7681f673bf0514e114/investigation/broader-evidence/faa-norad-chronology-routing-and-log-review-2026-11-09/README.md)
+
+## What staff asked and what the records report
+
+- **Flight 175.** The question sheet asks for the source of the **15939** entry and cites a NORAD release with **1243Z** and **SOURCE RADES**. It also asks whether the aircraft had been hijacked by then. The question and its quoted account remain distinct from the original log entry, operator and recipient. Questions p. 1 lines 28–35. [Source locators](SOURCE-PINS.json)
+- **Flight 77.** The sheet asks where the **0924** notification time used in the NORAD timeline and a May presentation came from and what information it represented. The previously acquired April FAA table gives `09:24*`. A shared minute in the two documents does not establish a common source or an actual notice. Questions p. 1 lines 36–41, April table p. 1 line 31. [April table and earlier chronology comparisons](https://github.com/AveLyra/911-conspiracy-investigation/blob/66e440317eb8a8cb44f9f678e352357e3e11342c/investigation/broader-evidence/faa-briefing-table-archive-counterparts-2026-11-09/README.md)
+- **Flight 93.** The January 18 **10:17 PM** reply gives **ZOB** and **10:06 a.m.** as the writer's tentative reading of the first call in the NEADS transcripts. The earlier email cites the absence of an earlier entry in the logs it discusses. Keep that scoped description beside the separate **1006** staff account of Commission feedback on the DEN line, the April table's `10:08*` and its narrative's qualified **0916:00**. Neither the source logs nor the exact transcript and scope of the writer's review were verified here. Team 8 p. 3 lines 5 and 11–13, p. 4 lines 2–9, item 497 p. 4 lines 24–28. [Source locators](SOURCE-PINS.json)
+
+## Verification and next records
+
+Seven source hash, CRC and page-count sets and 50 fixed anchors passed using PyMuPDF 1.27.2.2. The scope was 29 new pages screened in text and seven selected image regions read. Two scoped text-layer comparisons across the separate archive copies scored **0.942529** and **0.880546** after tokenization. The copies supply overlapping messages and different packaging. Matching material from the same email is one report. An additional marked interview copy stayed within local handling and supplied no finding. [Verification fields](SOURCE-PINS.json)
+
+Seek an authorized release of the actual tab 5–7 logs and the **53160** item, plus the dated production cover and Commission intake manifest for Document Request 4. The decisive comparison is between the exact entry and its author, recipients, field definitions and source clock. For Flight 93, pair the original transcript passage and recording with the dated exchange about the DEN line. The April 27 fax 2511 first page and dispatch or receipt record, the corrected May 2003 answer and recipient acknowledgment, and the signed administrative and recommendation outcomes remain unacquired.
